You have findings consistent with idiopathic intracranial hypertension, sometimes called pseudotumor cerebri. This condition occurs when the pressure of the fluid surrounding the brain becomes elevated without the presence of a tumor or mass.\
\
Increased pressure inside the skull can affect the optic nerves and cause swelling of the optic nerve heads. This can lead to symptoms such as headaches, blurred vision, episodes of temporary vision dimming, double vision, or ringing in the ears that pulses with the heartbeat.\
\
Because increased pressure can affect the optic nerves and potentially threaten vision, careful medical evaluation and monitoring are important.\
\
